WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.919
Perhaps the biggest nightmare begins

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:03.869
right here, looming over the

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.519
industrial revolution, the

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.469
displacement of millions of workers

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:09.839
and the loss of

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.029
huge number of jobs.

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:13.349
It's funny because everybody's like,

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:14.699
"Oh, robots are stealing our jobs.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:15.899
And I'm like, "No, humans have been

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.759
doing robot jobs." It's

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.469
tragic that we're having humans

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.329
perform such basic tasks all day." I

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:23.519
think humans have such capabilities

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.949
that they should be reserved for

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:27.869
high value things that only

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:29.409
humans can do. If a machine can do

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:30.449
it, then the machine should do it.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:31.859
So I think that's what's going to

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.929
happen. Just spend your entire

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:35.819
day in flow state and do

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:37.859
whatever you do best, and

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.149
then a machine should pick your

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:40.619
whole the logistical work around

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:41.429
that.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:43.319
Software engineer Flo Crivello is

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.359
the founder and CEO of Lindy,

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.129
which is part of the new Artificial

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:48.809
Intelligence gold rush that could

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:49.679
upend the U.S.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:50.639
labor market.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:51.839
To better understand how that

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:53.489
disruption will play out, it's worth

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:54.749
looking at how the intersection of

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:56.399
computing and telecommunications

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.199
have already impacted how Americans

00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.919
spend their working hours.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:01.139
A.I. furthers what the venture

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:02.369
capitalist Marc Andreessen has

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:04.109
described as "software eating the

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.399
world."

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:06.719
Even as the number of employed

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:08.729
Americans grew by 16% from

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:11.069
2000 to 2021, people

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:12.809
working as executive secretaries and

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:14.459
executive administrative assistants

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:16.979
fell by 66%

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:18.719
because of tools like voicemail,

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:20.669
calendar apps, web browsers and

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:22.109
dictation software.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:23.699
Lindy could drive that number down

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.409
even further by creating an AI

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:26.879
personal secretary that can book

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.739
travel, take notes during meetings

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.059
and handle basic email

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:31.349
correspondence.

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:32.759
The long term vision is to basically

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:34.109
automate all knowledge work, so we

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:35.969
really think of her as an

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:37.409
AI employee of sorts.

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:38.939
Over the same time period as the

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:40.649
overall labor market grew, the

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:42.449
number of travel agents plummeted by

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:44.309
70%, thanks in part

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:46.379
to direct to consumer booking sites.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.269
Now, tools like Tripnotes AI

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:49.739
can build a full travel itinerary

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:51.329
without the hassle of price matching

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:52.919
or spending hours reading up on

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.749
whether the ancient Athens store or

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:56.369
the gourmet food tasting are the

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:58.289
better use of an afternoon.

00:01:58.290 --> 00:01:59.249
The number of professional

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:00.329
photographers in the U.S.

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:02.669
has fallen by 41% since 2000

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:03.839
because of smartphones and

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.789
ubiquitous digital cameras.

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:07.769
Now, startups like Head Headshot Pro

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:09.209
could drive that down with its AI

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:10.859
generated professional headshots

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:12.749
such as this one, which cost me

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:14.849
$29 versus the roughly

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:16.859
$700 that it cost to hire

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:18.719
a human photographer here where I

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:20.519
live in Washington, D.C..

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.319
AI tools that already exists could

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.569
automate 25% of the tasks

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:26.609
American workers do, according

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:28.409
to a recent report by Goldman Sachs,

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:29.849
which will free up humans to do

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:31.229
other things.

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:32.819
The prediction that I will put us

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:34.079
all out of work is based on a

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:35.969
fallacy, and we can expect

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:37.769
total employment to continue rising

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:39.329
even as technology wipes out

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:40.889
specific jobs.

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:42.149
It's a process of "creative

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:43.649
destruction," to use the phrase of

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:45.359
the economist Joseph Schumpeter,

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:46.709
which explains why whole new

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:47.759
industries emerge following the

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:49.139
disappearance of ice cutters,

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:50.489
blacksmiths, harness makers,

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:51.719
wheelwrights, ditch diggers,

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:52.720
weavers, telegraphist, pinsetters,

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:55.289
lamp lighters, elevator operators,

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:56.399
lectors, clock keepers,

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.139
projectionist, milkmen and log

00:02:58.140 --> 00:02:59.969
drivers. In the process, we became

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:01.349
profoundly richer.

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:02.849
Technology frees up resources to

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:04.259
create jobs that people in the past

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:05.699
could never have imagined.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:07.529
And no, this time is

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:09.149
not different.

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:10.799
People mistakenly believe that there

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.749
is a fixed amount of work

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:14.579
to be performed in the economy.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:16.439
The reality of it is that

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:18.389
human needs and wants or

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:20.309
infinite the lifestyle

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:22.079
that you and I live is actually

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:23.609
better than the lifestyle of Louis

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:25.889
the 16th, and we still want more.

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:27.719
In the beginning of the 1900s,

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.029
something like 90% of the population

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:31.049
were farmers.

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:32.879
Since then, we went down and

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:35.009
very quickly to 5% of the

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:36.269
active population or something like

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:37.895
that. And yet we don't massive

00:03:37.896 --> 00:03:38.896
unemployment, very

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:41.968
far from it in the U.S.

00:03:41.969 --> 00:03:42.989
Since 2000, factory automation has
driven

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:45.119
down manufacturing employment, even

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:46.019
though the U.S.

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:47.879
is producing more stuff than ever

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:48.719
before.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:50.159
On the other side of the ledger,

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:51.629
software development has been one of

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:53.069
the fastest growing professions

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:54.899
since 2000, increasing by

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:57.029
143%

00:03:57.030 --> 00:03:58.769
and outpacing the growth of overall

00:03:58.770 --> 00:03:59.999
employment.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.039
That trend will probably continue.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:03.569
AI won't replace coders, but it will

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:04.649
make it possible for the less

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:06.509
technically sophisticated to drive

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:08.549
more sophisticated applications.

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:09.839
And this will enable founders to

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:11.339
bootstrap an idea with minimal

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:12.119
backing.

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:13.499
Similar to how cloud computing

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:14.909
enabled tech startups to bring their

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:16.289
products to market without their own

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:17.969
backend infrastructure.

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:19.379
Crivello predicts that there will be

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:20.879
demand for software applications to

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.709
fit every conceivable need

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:24.599
as the economy moves toward more

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:26.519
personalized everything.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:28.739
When you ask something

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:30.481
to Lindy, the way she performs the

00:04:30.482 --> 00:04:31.954
task actually is that she writes a

00:04:31.955 --> 00:04:32.984
little piece of code to perform the
task for you.

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:36.809
For example, if you say "Hey help

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:37.810
me find

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:39.749
30 minutes with Bob next week, she's
going to write a

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:40.979
piece of code to be like, "I'm going

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:41.939
to look at your calendar.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:43.179
I'm going to pull availabilities in

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:44.161
with equal in an email.

00:04:44.162 --> 00:04:46.145
I'm going to send them to Bob and

00:04:46.146 --> 00:04:46.979
I'm gonna wait for his reply." So

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:48.269
all of that is a mini piece of code.

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:50.069
And I think that's amazingly

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:52.229
interesting because I always compare

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:54.419
it to the Industrial Revolution

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:55.889
where it's like before the

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:58.019
Industrial Revolution, certain goods

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:00.119
like caps of pens were very

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:00.989
expensive.

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:03.119
One cap was probably

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:04.408
of the order of like 100 or 500

00:05:04.409 --> 00:05:05.409
dollars, right?

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:07.589
And after the revolution,

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:09.539
these goods became so cheap that

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:11.129
we started to have single use

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:12.809
versions of them.

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:14.009
And so, you know, we have like the

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:15.869
right solo caps and like the bic

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:17.729
pens and they're just so cheap

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:19.049
that you can't even refill of bic

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:20.719
pen. It's just cheaper to buy a new

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:21.720
one than to replace the ink.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:23.231
And so here it's the same thing

00:05:23.232 --> 00:05:25.319
historically, code

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:26.609
has been very expensive.

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:28.619
One app has cost of the order of

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:31.679
$10,000 to $100,000

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:32.908
with the AI cognitive revolution

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:36.059
code is becoming so cheap that it

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:37.589
can be disposable in single use.

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:40.169
And so it costs on the order of

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:41.069
one cent to build an application.

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:42.239
And so effectively you're going to

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:44.519
live in a world where bespoke

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:46.289
mini applications are built for

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:46.356
every little use case that you have
throughout your day. And that's what

00:05:46.357 --> 00:05:47.357
Lindy's doing effectively.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:52.349
If we think about generative AI

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:54.269
right now, I can create

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:55.889
a photo of, you know, Gandalf

00:05:55.890 --> 00:05:57.719
wearing a pink shirt in a

00:05:57.720 --> 00:05:59.249
silly hat in seconds.

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:00.719
Alex Banks writes the newsletter

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:01.919
Through the Noise and is the

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:03.869
co-founder of Tribescaler, a tool

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:05.309
that uses an AI algorithm to

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:07.229
generate viral social media hooks.

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:08.969
And all of these create a task that

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:10.949
would take a Photoshop expert

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:12.149
hours.

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:13.829
I can now do with a very simple

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:14.729
prompt.

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:17.009
It is leveling the playing field for

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:18.839
creative output, where

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:20.009
those who weren't creative, those

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:21.659
who couldn't tell stories, now can,

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:23.129
and those who were creative are only

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:24.389
getting even better.

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:25.619
I see A.I.

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:27.479
as on demand

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:28.379
intelligence, right?

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:30.269
It's way cheaper

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:32.159
and way faster than what you

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:33.179
can get from humans.

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:35.849
Just because tools like Amper can

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:37.259
generate tracks like this one with

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:39.059
the simple prompt of exciting, fast

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:40.949
paced and a little bit suspenseful.

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:42.689
That doesn't mean that AI will put

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:44.669
the nearly 10,000 Americans working

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:46.589
as music directors and composers

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:47.639
out of a job.

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:49.649
Rather, it'll mean a lot more

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:51.539
individually tailored music.

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:53.369
Tools like Amper will do some of the

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:55.169
work, but it will help composers

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:57.149
work faster, better and free

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:59.279
them up for higher level tasks.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:01.319
Radio GPT can pick songs, write

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:02.519
scripts and have a realistic

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:03.779
sounding voice read it.

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:05.129
But the demand for human curated

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:06.869
music won't disappear.

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:08.759
The Internet destroyed album sales,

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:10.109
which were once a major source of

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:11.459
revenue, but the number of

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:12.929
professional musicians has still

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:14.879
grown by 76%

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:16.379
since 2000.

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:17.879
Banks and Crivello are working on

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:19.739
systems that utilize GPT-4

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:21.239
and artificial intelligence to do

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:23.039
things that a generation ago would

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:25.319
have been unimaginable.

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:27.179
We have the tools that will

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:29.399
augement human potential.

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:31.349
So Tribe Scaling itself helps

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:32.350
you go viral on Twitter. It's your

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:35.519
AI writing assistant and

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:36.689
personal collaborative.

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:38.819
We use GPT-4 in really two settings.

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:40.679
Number one is to help you by a great

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:42.689
hook. So a hook is the number

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:44.069
one factor in whether your piece of

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:45.959
content succeeds or sits in the

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:47.429
dock. And so the hook is the

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:49.049
absolute critical factor.

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:50.909
And so we use GPT-4 with fine

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:53.429
tune prompts to use

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:55.259
vetted viral structures to help you

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:56.039
write better.

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:57.629
Banks is also currently working on a

00:07:57.630 --> 00:07:59.459
project called Aloy, which he thinks

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:00.629
will improve on not taking

00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:02.549
applications that are being marketed

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:03.959
as a second brain.

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:06.119
If we look at Notion, it's

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:09.179
a wonderful platform to

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:11.519
store all of your personal knowledge

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:13.349
and it's fairly good at

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:14.879
retreating with some search.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:16.199
It does a fairly good job, but it

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:18.299
isn't. It isn't entirely robust.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:19.829
And then you have other platforms,

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:21.449
right, on that capture phase, such

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:23.369
as say Things Three.

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:25.319
That is a wonderful quick capture on

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:27.329
the desktop where

00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:29.309
I can hit control spacebar

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:31.076
and I can immediately capture a

00:08:31.077 --> 00:08:32.668
thought. But then it's stored in a

00:08:32.669 --> 00:08:34.739
central repository and it's up to me

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:36.509
to relocate that thought.

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:37.769
What we're doing with Aloy is

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:39.149
essentially going, okay, how can we

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:40.319
complete that cycle for an

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:42.149
individual and build a

00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:43.979
second brain for you so you can

00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:45.929
access your creativity on

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:46.889
demand?

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:48.959
If I'm here and I'm wanting to

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:51.419
write a tweet, say, and

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:53.579
six months ago I had a conversation

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:54.899
with my friend Natalie and we were

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:56.459
chatting about AI and there was a

00:08:56.460 --> 00:08:57.929
wonderful insight that I got from

00:08:57.930 --> 00:08:59.129
that conversation, and I added it to

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:00.239
my second brain.

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:01.240
All on GPT-3.

00:09:03.030 --> 00:09:04.229
Now, when I go to write that tweet

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:06.359
and I start to write about GPT-3,

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:08.189
all of a sudden, what Aloy can do

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:10.229
is it could resurface that idea

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:11.879
for you dynamically.

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:13.289
How we think about how that's going

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:15.509
to propel productivity is

00:09:15.510 --> 00:09:16.709
really, really vast.

00:09:16.710 --> 00:09:18.539
And something that I'm massively

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:20.129
excited for.

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:21.479
Change is scary.

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:23.519
I think we all are about to see

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:25.469
maybe the biggest change in

00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:26.969
human history.

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:28.379
That sounds like hyperbole, but I

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:30.029
really believe that. People are

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:32.189
comparing AI to smartphones

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:33.329
or the internet.

00:09:33.330 --> 00:09:34.409
I think it's much closer to, I think
it's Larry Samuels who

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:36.029
said, it's much to the invention of

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:37.319
fire or the wheel.

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:38.669
Though the Goldman Sachs report

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:40.319
predicts that 30% of industries

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:42.389
won't be impacted by AI at all.

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:43.679
Some of these tools will free up

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:45.419
human labor for service occupations

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:47.069
like that of food, which hasn't been

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:48.869
fundamentally altered by computing

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:50.909
and has grown nearly 37%

00:09:50.910 --> 00:09:52.769
since 2000, outpacing the

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:54.299
growth of overall employment.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:55.889
The same goes for taxi drivers and

00:09:55.890 --> 00:09:56.890
personal trainers.

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:00.278
As Crivello points out, we're

00:10:00.279 --> 00:10:02.409
hoarding too much human labor

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:04.569
in existing industries, especially

00:10:04.570 --> 00:10:05.799
those controlled by the public

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:06.669
sector.

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:08.529
For example, the federal government

00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:10.569
wants to hire 87,000 new

00:10:10.570 --> 00:10:12.519
IRS agents when those same people

00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:13.539
could be producing goods and

00:10:13.540 --> 00:10:15.309
services that other humans actually

00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:16.389
value.

00:10:16.390 --> 00:10:17.529
Health care administrators have

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:19.239
grown in number since 2000, partly

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:20.709
because of all the red tape that

00:10:20.710 --> 00:10:21.939
came with Obamacare.

00:10:21.940 --> 00:10:23.049
Technology might have already

00:10:23.050 --> 00:10:24.189
started shrinking the education

00:10:24.190 --> 00:10:25.569
labor force and new tools like

00:10:25.570 --> 00:10:26.814
Course AI that can design syllabi,

00:10:26.815 --> 00:10:29.229
or Cognii, which offers one-on-one

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:30.729
AI tutoring, will drive further

00:10:30.730 --> 00:10:32.679
productivity gains in the sector.

00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:34.689
Remaking how we work is a core part

00:10:34.690 --> 00:10:36.579
of every tech revolution, and it's

00:10:36.580 --> 00:10:37.899
through this process that we've

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:39.699
profoundly driven up our standard of

00:10:39.700 --> 00:10:41.799
living. Let the robots have

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:42.800
their jobs.

